		{"id":"268b0d7355ee97b8","x":-620,"y":-340,"width":320,"height":340,"type":"text","text":"https://store.roboticsbd.com/development-boards/948-esp32-esp-32s-30p-nodemcu-development-board-wireless-wifi-robotics-bangladesh.html\n\n![[Pasted image 20260730215049.png]]"},
		{"id":"53adf0a56e05438c","x":-200,"y":-340,"width":300,"height":340,"type":"text","text":"https://store.roboticsbd.com/internet-of-things-iot/2271-esp32-30p-development-expansion-board-robotics-bangladesh.html\n\n![[Pasted image 20260730215155.png]]"},
		{"id":"7e680aaaebea6e97","x":300,"y":-360,"width":312,"height":340,"type":"text","text":"https://rcgearbd.com/product/sonoff-ip66-waterproof-junction-box-waterproof-case-water-resistant-shell-compatible-with-basic-dual-pow-rf-th16-smart-switches/\n\n\n![[Pasted image 20260730215815.png]]"},
		{"id":"4cbcc6bc01725de6","x":-740,"y":40,"width":625,"height":6070,"type":"text","text":"Yes. After comparing the actual existing builds, I would change our earlier design. The ESP32-S3 main board, S3 cameras, ADS1115, dual expensive air sensors, RTC and OLED are unnecessary for the first version.\n\nThe revised system is considerably cheaper while retaining the important safety features.\n\n## What the existing projects use\n\n| Project | Main components | What we should copy | What we should change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Oasis Terrarium](https://oasis-terrarium.com/docs/electronics/) | ESP32-C3, SHT30, two 24V fans, five Cree LEDs, custom LED driver, raw mist disc and custom driver, 18V supply | Separate the sensor from the hot grow light; use fans, Wi‑Fi and PWM | Its custom PCBs and raw mist-driver circuit are unnecessarily difficult |\n| [ESP32 Smart Terrarium](https://www.hackster.io/rau7han/iot-based-esp32-smart-terrarium-19ed25) | ESP32, DHT11, soil sensor, OLED, RGB LEDs, three relays, fan, pump, mist maker | Automatic/manual modes, dashboard, hysteresis and fail-safe logic | DHT11 is inaccurate, RGB ring is not a proper grow light, exposed mains supply is unsuitable near water |\n| [Arduino Forum terrarium](https://forum.arduino.cc/t/building-and-automating-terrarium-with-esp32s/1068377) | Multiple ESP32s, OLED, several sensors, mist, intake/exhaust fans, lights, reservoir and drip-wall pump | Misting cooldowns and non-blocking control | Multiple main controllers make wiring and programming harder |\n| [EnvControl](https://blackdevice.com/project/envcontrol-smart-climate-for-living-habitats/) | ESP32, BME280, DS18B20 sensors, relays, dimmers and web interface | BME280, day/night profiles and local web control | Its AC TRIAC system and custom PCB are unnecessary for us |\n\n## Revised controller choice\n\nBuy one [ESP32 DevKit V1 – approximately ৳485](https://techshopbd.com/product/esp32-devkit-v1).\n\nThis ordinary ESP32 is the better choice here because:\n\n- It is extremely common in Bangladesh.\n- It has more usable pins than the small ESP32-C3 boards.\n- It is cheaper than the ESP32-S3.\n- Almost every sensor library and tutorial supports it.\n- It can run the automatic controls and local web dashboard.\n- The cameras will have their own processors, so the main board needs no PSRAM.\n\nChoose the 38-pin version with an onboard PCB antenna. Do not choose the WROOM-32UE external-antenna version unless an antenna is included.\n\n## Cheaper revised shopping list\n\n### Control and cameras\n\n| Qty | Component | Approximate cost |\n|---:|---|---:|\n| 1 | [ESP32 DevKit V1](https://techshopbd.com/product/esp32-devkit-v1) | ৳485 |\n| 2 | [Standard ESP32-CAM with OV2640](https://www.bdtronics.com/esp32-cam-wifi-bluetooth-camera-module-ov2640-2mp-development-board.html) | ৳820 each |\n| 1 | [ESP32-CAM-MB Type-C programmer](https://store.roboticsbd.com/internet-of-things-iot/3602-esp32-cam-mb-type-c-download-module-for-esp32-cam-robotics-bangladesh.html) | ৳330 |\n\nThe programmer is reusable: program camera 1, remove it, and then program camera 2.\n\nThese standard ESP32-CAM boards save roughly ৳1,500 compared with two ESP32-S3 camera boards. They are sufficient for two low-resolution live feeds or periodic photographs.\n\nBuy one camera first, test its image and Wi‑Fi range, and then order the second from the same supplier/batch.\n\n### Sensors\n\n| Qty | Component | Approximate cost |\n|---:|---|---:|\n| 1 | [BME280 air temperature/humidity sensor](https://store.roboticsbd.com/pressure-flow/3263-bme280-i2c-pressure-humidity-temperature-sensor-module-robotics-bangladesh.html) | ৳420–550 |\n| 3 | [Capacitive soil-moisture sensor](https://store.roboticsbd.com/temperature-humidity/1042-capacitive-soil-moisture-sensor-robtoics-bangladesh.html) | ৳189 each |\n| 1 | [BH1750 light sensor](https://store.roboticsbd.com/sensors/429-bh1750fvi-digital-light-intensity-sensor-robtoics-bangladesh.html) | ৳235 |\n| 1 | [Waterproof DS18B20 temperature probe](https://store.roboticsbd.com/robotics-parts/414-waterproof-ds18b20-digital-thermal-probe-or-sensor-robotics-bangladesh.html) | ৳209 |\n| 1 | [Horizontal float switch](https://store.roboticsbd.com/sensors/1677-52mm-pp-liquid-water-level-sensor-horizontal-float-switch-down-robotics-bangladesh.html) | ৳299 |\n| 1 | [Leak sensor](https://store.roboticsbd.com/sensors/683-water-level-sensor-depth-of-detection-water-sensor-for-arduinoroboticsbangladesh.html) | ৳49 |\n\nUse two soil sensors and keep the third as a spare. The cheap sensors are acceptable if you:\n\n- Keep their electronic components above the soil.\n- Coat the upper PCB/components with epoxy or conformal coating.\n- Do not coat the long capacitive sensing area.\n- Calibrate every sensor separately in dry and fully wet soil.\n\nWe can add the more accurate SHT35 later if the BME280 proves insufficient. One BME280 is adequate for the prototype.\n\n### Water, mist and ventilation\n\n| Qty | Component | Approximate cost |\n|---:|---|---:|\n| 1 | [Complete 24V mist maker with adapter](https://www.bdtronics.com/24v-color-changing-ultrasonic-mist-maker-fogger.html) | ৳948 |\n| 1 | [12V peristaltic dosing pump](https://store.roboticsbd.com/motor/1964--small-peristaltic-dosing-pump-12v-silicone-tubing-robotics-bangladesh.html) | ৳850 |\n| 1 | [12V IP68 moat circulation pump](https://store.roboticsbd.com/motor/1872-mini-dc12v-brushless-pump-ad20p-1230a-48w-ip68-hmax-300cm-qmax-240-l-h-robotics-bangladesh.html) | ৳750 |\n| 2 | [12V 40mm fans](https://store.roboticsbd.com/3d-printer/727-12v-mini-cooling-fan-40x40x10mm-robotics-bangladesh.html) | ৳150 each |\n\nInstead of intake fan + exhaust fan + circulation fan, use:\n\n- One exhaust fan with a passive screened intake opening.\n- One internal circulation fan.\n\nThat is simpler and more reliable. Automatic servo-controlled vents can be added later.\n\nKeep the peristaltic pump even though cheaper pumps exist. It gives repeatable watering quantities and greatly reduces accidental siphoning and overwatering.\n\n### Switching and power\n\n| Qty | Component | Purpose |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 1 | [Four-channel 3.3V-compatible MOSFET board](https://store.roboticsbd.com/electronics-module/2940-mosfet-f5305s-4-channel-pulse-trigger-switch-controller-robotics-bangladesh.html) | Root pump, circulation pump and two fans |\n| 2 | [D4184 MOSFET modules](https://store.roboticsbd.com/electronics-module/2757-d4184-mosfet-control-module-robotics-bangladesh.html) | Grow light and 24V fogger |\n| 10 | [1N5819 protection diodes](https://store.roboticsbd.com/diode/3376-1n5819-schottky-diode-40v-1a-robotics-bangladesh.html) | Motor/fan protection; includes spares |\n| 1 | [Fixed 12V 5A adapter](https://store.roboticsbd.com/chargers-balancer/3020-12v-5a-power-supply-adapter-robotics-bangladesh.html) | All 12V equipment |\n| 1 | [XL4015 5A buck converter](https://store.roboticsbd.com/robotics-parts/395--xl4015-dc-dc-step-down-adjustable-power-supply-module-5a-max-robotics-bangladesh.html) | Converts 12V to 5V for ESP32s |\n| 3 | [5.5×2.1mm barrel connector sets](https://store.roboticsbd.com/tools-accessories/1587-barrel-connector-female-robotics-bangladesh.html) | Adapter connections |\n\nThe mist maker already includes its own 24V adapter.\n\nBefore connecting any ESP32, use a multimeter to adjust the XL4015 output to exactly `5.0V`. Add a 470–1000µF capacitor near each camera’s 5V input to prevent camera brownouts.\n\n## Revised cost\n\nThe revised core electronics come to approximately:\n\n| Section | Approximate cost |\n|---|---:|\n| Main controller, two cameras and programmer | ৳2,455 |\n| Sensors | ৳1,750 |\n| Mist maker, pumps and fans | ৳2,850 |\n| MOSFET drivers and protection | ৳900 |\n| 12V adapter, buck converter and connectors | ৳750 |\n| **Estimated electronic total** | **৳8,700–৳9,000** |\n\nThis excludes:\n\n- Grow light\n- Enclosure/acrylic\n- Tree, soil and hardscape\n- Tubing\n- Electrical box, wiring, fuses and cable glands\n\nA realistic complete system excluding the tree and glass enclosure should be around ৳11,000–৳14,000, depending mainly on the grow light.\n\n## Revised connection map\n\n### Sensors\n\n| ESP32 pin | Connection |\n|---|---|\n| GPIO21 | BME280 and BH1750 SDA |\n| GPIO22 | BME280 and BH1750 SCL |\n| GPIO34 | Soil sensor 1 analog output |\n| GPIO35 | Soil sensor 2 analog output |\n| GPIO32 | Leak sensor analog output |\n| GPIO4 | DS18B20 data, with 4.7kΩ pull-up to 3.3V |\n| GPIO27 | Low-water float switch, configured `INPUT_PULLUP` |\n\nPower the BME280, BH1750 and soil sensors from `3.3V`, not 5V.\n\n### Controlled outputs\n\n| ESP32 pin | Device |\n|---|---|\n| GPIO16 | Root-watering peristaltic pump |\n| GPIO17 | Moat circulation pump |\n| GPIO18 | Exhaust fan |\n| GPIO19 | Internal circulation fan |\n| GPIO23 | Grow light |\n| GPIO25 | Mist maker |\n\nConnect GPIO16–19 to the four-channel MOSFET board. Connect GPIO23 and GPIO25 to the two individual D4184 modules.\n\n### Power distribution\n\n```text\n12V 5A adapter\n   ├── Four-channel MOSFET → pumps and fans\n   ├── D4184 → 12V grow light\n   └── XL4015 set to 5.0V\n          ├── Main ESP32 VIN/5V\n          ├── Camera 1 5V\n          └── Camera 2 5V\n\nIncluded 24V adapter\n   └── Separate D4184 → mist maker\n```\n\nThe two cameras have no data connection to the main controller. All three ESP32 boards join the same 2.4GHz Wi‑Fi network.\n\n## Components removed from the original list\n\nWe can safely omit these from version one:\n\n- ESP32-S3 main board\n- Two expensive ESP32-S3 camera boards\n- SHT35 sensor\n- Second air sensor\n- ADS1115 ADC\n- DS3231 RTC—use internet NTP time\n- OLED—use the web dashboard\n- MicroSD logging module\n- Second float switch\n- Third ventilation fan\n- Vent servos and PCA9685\n\nWe are not removing safety-critical parts: low-water cutoff, leak detection, separate root watering, motor protection and external low-voltage power remain.\n\nThe only component I would not finalize yet is the grow light. Its wattage depends on the enclosure’s inside width, depth, height and the selected tree. Buying that before those measurements could either waste money or leave the plant under-lit."}
	],
